<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Memory. Both keys are supported on visionOS, neither needs Apple's approval
  (Xcode's own capability database: supportedSDKs includes VISION_OS,
  distributionApprovalRequired false), and automatic signing adds them to the
  App ID on the next build with -allowProvisioningUpdates.

  We want them because this app is not shaped like a normal one: it hosts a
  whole other runtime. Measured on device at three swapchain stages, the
  footprint is ~3.5 GB — IL2CPP's heap, Unity's assets and textures, ANGLE, and
  ~490 MB of eye textures, with a transient spike at every loading transition
  where two generations of the eye swapchain are briefly live at once.

  increased-memory-limit
      Raises the jetsam limit — how much the app may use before the OS kills it.
      This is the one that matters for the transition spike.

  extended-virtual-addressing
      Raises the limit on address SPACE rather than resident memory. A separate
      ceiling, and one this design leans on independently of footprint: the
      guest ELF images are mmapped, the veneer pool and stub cells are mapped,
      ANGLE maps its own, and the synthetic /proc tree is materialised on disk
      and mapped too. Address space is cheap to consume and expensive to
      discover you have run out of.

  NOT increased-debugging-memory-limit: iOS only, no VISION_OS in its
  supportedSDKs, and an entitlement the platform does not know is a
  provisioning failure rather than a no-op.

  ===========================================================================
  networking.multicast, and this one is NOT like the two above.

  Steam Link discovers hosts by UDP BROADCAST to port 27036 (SL-5 measured it).
  On this platform broadcast and multicast are gated behind an entitlement that
  Apple grants by REQUEST, not by ticking a capability box:

      https://developer.apple.com/contact/request/networking-multicast

  So it differs from the memory pair in the one way that matters operationally:
  they are self-service, this is not. Until the request is granted, attaching it
  fails the BUILD — the profile cannot carry an entitlement the App ID has not
  been granted — which is why KLEPTON_MULTICAST=0 exists and why the failure is
  named in gen_xcodeproj.py rather than left to be read off a signing error.

  It is also genuinely broad, and worth being honest about: it lets the app send
  to and receive from every device on the network. We want it for exactly one
  thing — the discovery sweep that populates the computer list — and the rest of
  the session is ordinary unicast to the one host that was paired with.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription is the OTHER half and is free; see Info.plist.
  Neither substitutes for the other: without the usage string there is no
  permission at all, and without this entitlement the permission does not extend
  to broadcast.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.networking.multicast</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
